<h2>The discretionary gate</h2>
<p>Article 136 is not an appeal; it is a petition for the Court's leave to appeal. In matrimonial matters the gate is narrower in practice than in many commercial fields, for a structural reason: family cases are fact-heavy, and the Supreme Court does not sit as a third court of facts. A party aggrieved by a finding of cruelty or desertion, a quantum of maintenance, or a custody schedule must show something more than disagreement — a misapplication of the legal standard, findings unsupported by the record, orders passed without jurisdiction or in breach of natural justice, or conflicting High Court positions on a question that needs settling.</p>

<h2>What matrimonial SLPs typically seek — and get</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Interim arrangements</strong><p>Protection against eviction from the matrimonial home, visitation pending proceedings, or conditions on execution of money decrees — short orders that stabilise the dispute.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Settlement under Article 142</strong><p>Composite orders dissolving the marriage, fixing one-time alimony and quashing cross-cases — the Supreme Court's most used matrimonial power.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Questions of law</strong><p>The occasional grant of leave on a genuine legal question — the scope of a maintenance provision, the standard for custody relocation, the interplay of overlapping statutes.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Correction of perversity</strong><p>Interference where the High Court's order is contradictory, ignores admitted material, or leaves a party remediless.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>What the Court rarely does</h2>
<p>Re-weigh evidence of cruelty; recalibrate maintenance fixed after proper affidavits of assets; disturb a child's settled arrangement absent demonstrated harm; or entertain SLPs against purely interlocutory family-court orders that can be corrected in the High Court. An SLP filed on such grounds is usually dismissed in limine — often with the one-line order that the Court is not inclined to interfere — which leaves the High Court order untouched and, under the doctrine of merger, decides nothing beyond that refusal.</p>

<h2>Strategy notes for the Delhi litigant</h2>
<p>First, exhaust the right forum. Many grievances dressed up as SLPs belong in a review before the High Court, an appeal within the family-courts structure, or an execution or modification application in the trial court — cheaper, faster, and without the risk of an early dismissal hardening the order.</p>
<p>Second, mind limitation and candour. The ninety-day period runs from the High Court order; condonation is discretionary; and suppression of parallel proceedings — a pending DV case, an execution, a quashing petition — is treated severely in a forum that insists on full disclosure.</p>
<p>Third, come with a workable proposal. Benches hearing matrimonial SLPs habitually explore settlement at the first listing. A party who arrives with a concrete structure — a figure for permanent alimony, a custody and visitation plan, a list of proceedings to be closed — controls that conversation; a party who arrives only with grievances follows it.</p>
<p>Finally, remember the transfer petition. For a spouse in Delhi facing proceedings in a distant State, the practical Supreme Court remedy is usually not an SLP against some order in that case, but a transfer petition seeking that the case itself be moved — a distinct, well-trodden jurisdiction with its own practice of video-conferencing directions and travel-cost conditions.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>This is a general explainer on Article 136 practice in matrimonial litigation. It is informational only; whether any particular order merits an SLP, a review, a transfer petition or nothing at all is a question of case-specific advice.</p>
</div>
